          "IN-HD-3 is a solidly Democratic district at 63-37, but the demographic profile beneath that margin reveals the specific constituency that must be retained and expanded to hold or grow it. At 35.57% Black, 44.18% white, and 17.09% Hispanic with a 39.4% renter rate and only 17.4% college attainment, this is an urban working-class district where renters and non-college-educated adults comprise the overwhelming majority of voters. The $52,796 median household income — below state median — and the 37.8-year median age together signal a constituency concentrated in the economic middle and lower-middle: people with stable employment but constrained household budgets, making rent and utility costs, healthcare access, and wages the primary material concerns. The 63% Democratic result reflects alignment on these kitchen-table issues, but the 37% Republican share and the district's economic profile together suggest that this margin depends entirely on whether the Democratic incumbent is perceived as delivering concrete improvements in housing costs, job quality, and economic security for renters and working families.",
