          { name: "Middle-Class Economic Security and Cost-of-Living Pressure", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $73,533 median household income with 38.6% college attainment and a 37.8-year median age, HD-5 is a solidly middle-class district where household economic stability is the baseline expectation — and where rising costs for healthcare, housing, and childcare create direct material pressure on the constituency that elected DeVon. The 55-45 result, while decisive, reflects a district where 45% of voters are actively looking for an alternative; understanding whether that 45% is driven by cost-of-living frustration, specific local service failures, or demographic shifts in voting preference is essential to consolidating and growing the current coalition. A representative who leads with concrete policies on healthcare affordability, housing costs, and wage sustainability addresses the economic anxiety that likely explains the 45% opposition in a district this economically comfortable." },
          { name: "College-Educated Professional Workforce Retention and Opportunity", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 38.6% college attainment rate and $73,533 income together indicate a district with a substantial professional workforce — teachers, healthcare workers, engineers, and other degree-holding residents whose career trajectories and family stability depend on both local economic opportunity and quality-of-life infrastructure. At a 37.8-year median age, this is a cohort in their peak earning and family-formation years, making education quality, housing affordability, and professional wages the concrete policy drivers of their electoral behavior. The 55-45 result in a college-heavy district suggests that the professional class is somewhat divided; engaging specifically on education investment, workforce development, and the economic competitiveness that retains degree-holding professionals in the district is a direct pathway to consolidation." },
          { name: "Renter Community Economic Mobility and Housing Affordability", tag: "careful", why: "At 29.1% renter rate in a district where median household income is $73,533, HD-5 has a meaningful constituency of renters — approximately 30% of the district — whose economic interests may diverge significantly from the homeowner majority. This renter cohort includes young professionals, service-sector workers, and families without home equity, for whom housing affordability, rental market regulation, and pathways to ownership are material concerns that may or may not align with homeowner-majority policies on property taxes and development. In a 55-45 competitive result, understanding whether renters are concentrated in the 45% opposition or distributed across both coalitions is crucial to understanding the district's true political geography; this group is large enough to swing the outcome if their specific concerns — housing cost, economic mobility, service access — are either addressed or neglected." },
          { name: "Treating the 45% Opposition as a Monolithic Out-Group", tag: "avoid", why: "A 55-45 result is decisive but not commanding — and the 45% opposition in a middle-class, college-educated district at $73,533 income cannot be dismissed as ideologically alien or unreachable. The opposition likely includes some combination of cost-of-living-frustrated middle-class households, renters concerned about housing affordability, and professionals whose service-sector or local-business interests place them at odds with the current representative's governance priorities. Any incumbent strategy that consolidates only the existing 55% and neglects to understand what specific issues or concerns animate the 45% is strategically incomplete and leaves the district vulnerable to a credible challenger who addresses those concerns." },
          { name: "Allowing Demographic Complexity to Become Invisible", tag: "avoid", why: "The four reported Census categories account for approximately 94.8% of HD-5's population, leaving a ~5.2% gap that is likely American Indian / Alaska Native residents — not a dominant demographic signal as in districts with 20%+ AIAN populations, but not trivial either. Additionally, the 6.27% Hispanic / Latino and 4.83% Asian populations are present in a district whose political narrative and constituent engagement often default to the white, college-educated, homeowner majority. Any representative whose constituent engagement, legislative focus, and community outreach are effectively limited to the white professional class is underserving approximately 16% of the district's demographic constituency and potentially ceding organizing opportunities to an opposition candidate who addresses these communities' specific concerns around economic mobility, education access, and cultural inclusion." }
