          { name: "Unopposed Incumbency & Hidden Opposition", tag: "lean-into", why: "Teshka won unopposed in 2024 with 100% of reported votes — a result that obscures rather than reveals the district's actual competitive terrain. In a district with 26.3% college attainment, $65,739 median income, and a 39.8-year median age that skews neither dramatically young nor old, an unopposed win tells you nothing about whether there is latent opposition, persuadable swing voters, or constituencies feeling underserved. The strategic imperative is to treat this seat not as safe but as uncontested — and to identify specifically what Democratic or independent opposition might emerge around, whether economic anxiety among renters (22%), education and opportunity concerns among the college-educated plurality (26.3%), or rural service delivery gaps. A representative who assumes unopposed means secure is vulnerable the moment a credible challenger appears." },
          { name: "Working-Class Economic Security & Renter Constituency", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $65,739 median household income with a 22% renter rate, HD-7 is a solidly working-class district where housing costs, healthcare access, and wage growth are material concerns for a significant portion of the constituency. The 73.12% white, 11.26% Hispanic, and 10.5% Black population together represent a multiethnic working-class community — not a homogeneous base. Renters at this income level are particularly vulnerable to housing cost inflation and economic shocks; a legislative agenda that addresses affordable housing, wage floors, healthcare affordability, and job quality in sectors that employ working-class families (agriculture, manufacturing, service work) is both substantively necessary and electorally potent. The unopposed 2024 result may mask frustration among this constituency if economic conditions deteriorate or if they perceive the incumbent as unresponsive to their specific material concerns." },
          { name: "Hispanic Community Engagement & Economic Opportunity", tag: "careful", why: "At 11.26% of the district, the Hispanic population is substantial enough to swing a close race but not so large as to be automatically visible in campaign strategy or governance priority. Hispanic residents in rural Oklahoma districts like HD-7 are concentrated in agricultural, construction, and service-sector employment — sectors where wage stagnation, workplace safety, and access to affordable healthcare are primary concerns. The strategic question is whether the incumbent has built authentic relationships with Hispanic community organizations, employers who hire Hispanic workers, and Hispanic households themselves — or whether Hispanic constituents are being assumed into a broader working-class narrative without specific attention. In an unopposed context, this invisibility may persist until a challenger explicitly targets Hispanic voters with concrete economic opportunity messaging." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed as Permanent Security", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed win in 2024 is a victory condition for a single cycle, not a durable electoral guarantee. In a district with only 26.3% college attainment and a working-class median income of $65,739, economic conditions and incumbent responsiveness are the primary determinants of electoral security. If the next cycle brings a credible challenger — whether Republican primary opposition, a Democratic recruit sensing opportunity, or an independent — the absence of any 2024 opposition message, constituent relationship-building, or demonstrated legislative accomplishment becomes a vulnerability. Any incumbent who spends the next two years assuming the unopposed result is permanent, rather than using it to deepen constituent relationships and build a legislative record, will be unprepared for a contested race." },
          { name: "Partisan Ideology as a Substitute for Economic Substance", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100% Republican result in an unopposed race invites the temptation to govern around abstract partisan positions rather than the concrete economic concerns of a working-class, multiethnic district where 22% are renters and median income is $65,739. In HD-7, messaging and legislation organized primarily around partisan conservatism — rather than around housing affordability, job quality, healthcare access, and wage growth — will fail to address the material concerns that could activate opposition. A challenger who can articulate a specific economic agenda for working families, renters, and Hispanic constituents will find receptive voters if the incumbent has spent two years delivering partisan rhetoric instead of tangible constituent solutions. The unopposed context makes this risk especially acute because there has been no 2024 campaign discipline forcing specificity." }
