          { name: "Property Tax and Fiscal Management for Affluent Homeowners", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 86.4% homeownership in a district where median household income is $104,325, property tax burden and whether state policy protects or threatens residential asset values is the primary material concern anchoring constituent loyalty. School funding adequacy, road maintenance, and municipal service quality directly affect property values in affluent suburbs, and a representative who delivers on these issues controls the economic security expectations of the constituency. Ignoring property tax and fiscal management in favor of broader ideological positioning is strategically self-defeating in a district where constituent loyalty is tied to tangible economic outcomes." },
          { name: "Professional Workforce and Small-Business Economic Opportunity", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 34.5% college-educated population in a $104K median income district indicates a significant professional and business-owner constituency for whom regulatory burden, occupational licensing reform, and tax efficiency directly affect income and business viability. This is not a working-class constituency evaluating on union wages or manufacturing jobs; it is a professional workforce evaluating on whether state policy removes obstacles to economic opportunity and whether tax policy rewards productivity rather than penalizing success. A legislator who can articulate specific, non-ideological answers to professional and small-business concerns on these dimensions is building durable constituent relationships that transcend simple partisan alignment." },
          { name: "Emerging Suburban Diversity and Minority Professional Communities", tag: "careful", why: "The 3.4% demographic gap in a district this sophisticated and affluent likely represents professional and immigrant communities whose presence, while small, signals ongoing suburban diversification and whose civic expectations include inclusive representation. While numerically limited in current constituency composition, these communities are often embedded in professional networks and corporate environments that amplify their influence beyond raw percentage representation, and their engagement or alienation can affect the reputation and perceived inclusivity of the community. A representative who recognizes this emerging diversity and engages these constituencies actively is building goodwill with communities whose numbers are growing; one who treats them as invisible risks losing credibility with the broader professional base that increasingly works alongside and values these communities." },
          { name: "Treating the Unopposed 2024 Result as Evidence of Invulnerability", tag: "avoid", why: "The 100% result reflects the absence of a Democratic candidate, not a referendum on the incumbent's governance or constituent satisfaction; unopposed elections are an unreliable metric for measuring actual voter loyalty or detecting emerging vulnerability. Affluent suburban constituencies are capable of demanding high standards from their representatives and will support a credible alternative if they conclude the incumbent is not delivering on material economic concerns or is taking them for granted. A legislator who relies on the unopposed margin as evidence of security rather than actively tending constituent relationships invites a competitive challenger in a future cycle when the opposition decides to invest." },
          { name: "Defaulting to Partisan Ideology Over Constituent Material Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "In a $104K median income, 86% homeowner district where voters are economically secure and professionally sophisticated, abstract partisan positioning about cultural issues or ideological principle is less likely to drive voting behavior than specific policy answers to property tax management, school funding, and regulatory efficiency. A representative who campaigns on partisan messaging rather than addressing the material governance concerns that directly affect constituent quality of life is leaving the base vulnerable to a challenger who leads with economic competence and fiscal responsibility regardless of partisan label. Affluent suburban constituencies are pragmatic; they reward representatives who deliver on tangible outcomes, not ideological performance." }
