          { name: "Hispanic Community Civic Integration & Economic Opportunity", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 10.95% Hispanic population represents roughly 1,095 constituents per 10,000 residents—a meaningful minority community whose economic and civic concerns can become invisible in a 100% unopposed result. In a district where the dominant story is Republican dominance, Hispanic constituents' concerns around labor protections, economic mobility, and civic integration are real and present—and a Democratic challenger offering specific attention to these issues has an opening. Active constituent service, workforce program access, and genuine engagement on immigration and economic opportunity issues with the Hispanic community are both a governing obligation and an electoral necessity." },
          { name: "Unopposed Victory as Strategic Vulnerability Rather Than Strength", tag: "careful", why: "The 100% unopposed result reflects the absence of organized Democratic challenge in 2024, not durable Republican structural dominance in a district where median income is below state levels and college attainment lags the state average. In a future cycle where Democratic organization materializes, the incumbent who has treated the seat as settled—neglecting younger renters, Hispanic constituents, or working-class economic anxiety—will find the 100% margin evaporating. The strategic task is to convert the unopposed victory into genuine constituent relationships, not assume it represents a structural floor." },
          { name: "Treating Below-Average Income as a Non-Issue", tag: "avoid", why: "At $74,826, IN-HD-22's median household income is meaningfully below state levels and below comparable districts, yet this economic constraint can be overlooked in a 100% unopposed victory. A representative who does not address the economic pressures facing working-class constituents—treating the seat as safely Republican and focusing rhetoric on abstract conservative positioning—will find constituent frustration building beneath the partisan surface. Economic anxiety is the most reliable driver of opposition mobilization when a challenger offers concrete alternatives." },
          { name: "Neglecting Younger, Renting Constituents as a Presumed Base", tag: "avoid", why: "With 24.2% renters and a 37.4 median age, IN-HD-22 has a meaningful population of younger, non-homeowning adults who do not have inherited partisan loyalty and who evaluate candidates on economic opportunity, housing affordability, and job creation—not party identification. An incumbent who treats these constituents as a presumed Republican base while directing resources elsewhere will find them receptive to Democratic outreach around housing affordability, student debt, or job creation. Younger renters in working-class districts are the most volatile swing constituency when opposition mobilizes around economic issues." }
