        issues: [
          { name: "University of Oklahoma Economic & Civic Anchor", tag: "lean-into", why: "HD-26's 65.6% college-educated rate is the highest signal of any district in this sequence, and the median age of 22.0 — by far the youngest — directly reflects OU's student body and young professional workforce. The university is the district's largest employer and economic driver, shaping housing demand, infrastructure needs, workforce development, and the fiscal health of Norman public schools. A representative who engages OU as an institutional partner on higher education funding, student affordability, housing policy, and the pipeline from undergraduate education to Oklahoma employment commands credibility with the constituency that actually shapes the district's character and future." },
          { name: "Young Adult Economic Mobility & Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 22.0 median age, 60% renter rate, and $44,434 household income paint a constituency of students, early-career professionals, and young families for whom housing costs relative to income and entry-level employment pathways are the primary economic concerns. A 65% Democratic performance in a state that is 68% Republican indicates that young adults in this district are actively choosing an alternative — likely driven by perceived misalignment between their economic constraints and the state's policy priorities on education funding, healthcare access, and wage-level employment. Candidates who address housing affordability, student debt burden, and wage-level job creation speak directly to what drives a young district's vote." },
          { name: "Asian American Community Integration", tag: "careful", why: "At 16.96%, the Asian American population is significantly above state and national baselines and represents the second-largest demographic group in the district after white residents. This community is likely concentrated in professional and academic roles connected to OU, small business ownership, and technical employment. While the district's 65% Democratic result suggests broad alignment with the current representative, Asian American constituents' specific concerns around higher education access, small business support, and workplace equity deserve active engagement rather than assumption of bloc solidarity. A representative who articulates specific understanding of this community's economic and civic role strengthens both governance and electoral durability." },
          { name: "Treating 65% as a Durable Coalition Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 65-35 margin in a university town is strong, but it rests on sustained alignment between the representative's performance and a young, educated, transient constituency that evaluates continuously on material outcomes rather than partisan habit. With 60% renter rate and a median age of 22, constituency turnover is rapid — new student cohorts and early-career professionals arriving and departing mean that the 65% must be actively re-earned with each election cycle rather than treated as a safe base. A representative who campaigns this district as though 65% is a durable majority and neglects to address the material concerns of young adults will discover that demographic change and natural turnover are sufficient to erode the margin." },
          { name: "Positioning OU as a Special Interest Rather Than a Civic Institution", tag: "avoid", why: "University funding, research priorities, and student life are not peripheral to Norman politics — they are central to the district's economic vitality, employment landscape, and civic identity. Any candidate whose rhetoric or voting record positions OU funding or university-related policy as a special interest competing with 'regular' constituents will find that framing actively disconnected from the material reality of a district where the university shapes housing costs, employment, infrastructure, and demographic character. In a 22.0-year-old median age district, the university is not an interest group — it is the constituent base." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "Norman: University Town with 65% Democratic Performance, 22-Year-Old Median Age, and Young Adults' Economic Mobility as the Swing Issue",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "HD-26 is fundamentally shaped by the University of Oklahoma and the young adult constituency it attracts and retains. The 65.6% college-educated rate — highest in any district in this sequence — and the 22.0 median age — by far the youngest — directly reflect OU's scale as the district's primary employer and economic anchor. Norman's economy, housing market, infrastructure investment, and civic priorities all flow from the university's needs and the demographic character of its students and young professional workers. The 65% Democratic performance in a state that is 68% Republican is not a partisan accident but a material reflection of young adults' evaluation of state governance on the issues that affect their lives: higher education funding and affordability, housing costs relative to entry-level wages, healthcare access, and whether the state's economic development priorities align with their employment prospects. A representative who engages OU as an institutional peer on these issues and articulates specific solutions to young adult economic mobility speaks the language of the district's actual constituency.",
          "The 60% renter rate and $44,434 median household income reveal a district where housing affordability and entry-level employment are the binding constraints on constituent wellbeing. Young adults in Norman — whether OU students completing degrees, recent graduates establishing careers, or young families building households — face a cost-of-living environment shaped by OU's demand for housing and the income levels of early-career professionals and service-sector workers. The district's Asian American population at 16.96%, significantly above baseline, is concentrated in professional, academic, and entrepreneurial roles connected to the university and Norman's knowledge economy, and deserves specific constituent attention rather than absorption into a broader 'young educated' narrative. The 35% opposition in this district is not a disengaged minority but an active, mobilized constituency — likely concentrated among older homeowners and business owners whose interests in property value and fiscal conservatism conflict with younger renters' priorities for service investment and affordable housing. A representative who neglects to understand what generates that 35% and speak directly to it will find the coalition fragile: with 60% renter rate and a transient population tied to the university's enrollment cycles, the 65% margin is strong but not durable without continuous engagement on the material concerns that drive young adults' votes."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Lead with OU partnership on higher education funding, student affordability, and workforce pipeline: the university is the district's primary employer and economic driver, and a representative who engages it as an institutional peer rather than a political proxy commands credibility with the constituency that shapes Norman's character.",
          "Address young adult economic mobility directly: housing affordability relative to entry-level wages, student debt burden, and the availability of career-track employment are the material concerns of a 22-year-old median age, 60% renter constituency — and these are the issues most likely driving the 65% Democratic alignment.",
          "Serve the Asian American community at 16.96% with specific constituent attention: this is the second-largest demographic group in the district and concentrated in professional, academic, and entrepreneurial roles; their economic and civic concerns deserve active engagement rather than assumption of bloc alignment.",
          "Treat the 35% opposition as a permanent feature to be engaged, not a temporary anomaly: with rapid demographic turnover from OU's enrollment cycles and a 60% renter base, the 65% is strong but requires continuous re-engagement on the young adult issues that matter most — treating this as a safe seat invites erosion."
        ]
