          { name: "Incumbent Complacency & Attrition Within the Supermajority", tag: "careful", why: "A 74% margin in a district this Republican can conceal attrition within the Republican base if the incumbent is perceived as taking the district for granted or prioritizing state-level conflicts over local constituent service. Primary challengers with superior local credentials, or simply lower turnout among existing Republican voters who feel unserved, can erode a supermajority that appears durable on the surface. The incumbent's strategic risk is not from the 26% opposition but from dissatisfaction within the 74%." },
          { name: "Treating the 26% Opposition as a Persuasion Target", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district that is 92% white, 75.9% homeowner, $81,300 median income, and 74% Republican, the 26% Democratic opposition reflects both the district's demographic homogeneity and the ceiling of Democratic support in affluent, homeowner-dominant Republican territory. Converting the 26% is not a feasible strategy; the Democratic margin is close to the structural floor. Spending political capital and messaging energy on persuading this 26% is a resource misallocation that distracts from the incumbent's real strategic task: consolidating the 74%." },
          { name: "State-Level Ideological Positioning as a Substitute for Local Governance", tag: "avoid", why: "In-HD-47 is not a district where voters prioritize state-level ideological positioning or culture-war issues as their primary concern. This is an affluent homeowner constituency focused on property taxes, schools, and local service quality. An incumbent who relies on state-level conservative positioning while neglecting local governance issues runs the risk of appearing distracted or ideologically driven rather than competently focused on constituent service. In this district's context, local governance performance is more valuable than state-level ideological alignment." }
