          "HD-52 presents a straightforward political picture on its surface: a 77-23 Republican victory in deeply red Oklahoma, a 92.5% white, 79% homeowner constituency with a median age of 39.4 and median household income of $70,031. The district is demographically homogeneous and electorally stable by contemporary Oklahoma standards. However, the four reported Census categories account for only 96.8% of the population, leaving a 3.2% gap — approximately 1,000-1,500 residents — whose identity and civic participation remain unaccounted for. While this gap is smaller than in districts with significant tribal presence, it represents a residual population whose needs and participation patterns deserve identification. More consequentially, the 19.4% college attainment rate is the lowest reported across the comparative sequence, signaling a constituency with limited educational attainment and the economic mobility constraints that follow.",
