          { name: "Understanding the 20% Opposition & Renter Constituency", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 22.7% renter rate and 20% Democratic opposition in an 80-20 district indicates a younger adult and lower-income constituency whose economic concerns—housing affordability, wage growth, healthcare access—diverge from the homeowner majority's priorities. In a seat with an 80% margin, the incumbent has room to lose: if challengers successfully mobilize the renter minority and any Native American community around economic mobility and opportunity concerns, the margin is arithmetically vulnerable. Understanding specifically who this 20% is, what their material concerns are, and why they voted against the incumbent is essential strategic intelligence." },
          { name: "Native American Community Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "The gap between the four reported Census categories (95.1%) and 100% suggests approximately 4.3% of the district's population is not accounted for—most likely American Indian / Alaska Native residents. While this is the smallest AIAN gap in the sequence, in a district this politically lopsided (80-20), even modest shifts in how a Native American constituency perceives the incumbent's responsiveness to tribal governance, healthcare access, or economic development concerns can affect downstream margin stability. Authentic engagement is not about winning this population away from the baseline; it is about avoiding the creation of an organized alternative." },
          { name: "Treating an 80-20 Seat as Untouchable", tag: "avoid", why: "An 80-20 margin, while genuinely strong, is not a mandate for complacency. The 20% opposition represents 25% as large as the majority itself; if that 20% becomes more organized, more mobilized, or perceives the incumbent as indifferent to their material concerns, the margin erodes quickly in the next competitive cycle. Incumbents in safe seats who treat the result as a reflection of personal popularity rather than alignment with the district's homeowner majority and existing party preference often find themselves vulnerable when a credible alternative emerges." },
          { name: "College-Educated Professional Base Development", tag: "avoid", why: "HD-55's 19.5% college attainment is well below state average, and the district's economy is oriented toward stable working-class and small-business employment rather than knowledge-sector development. Any incumbent who frames economic development strategy primarily around attracting college-educated professionals or knowledge-sector employers is speaking to a minority of the actual constituency and implicitly signaling that the district's existing working-class character is insufficient. This framing alienates the 80% majority who do not have college degrees and who value economic stability in their current industries over speculative transformation narratives." }
