          "The strategic vulnerability of HD-58 is not the current margin but the assumption of permanence that a 67% result can create. At $81,509 income and 29.8% college education, this is a constituency capable of ticket-splitting on issues: healthcare access and cost, property tax fairness, education funding, and whether state government is delivering competent administration are the issues that move affluent, educated voters. The 33% opposition—concentrated among renters, younger adults, and minority communities—is precisely the constituency most likely to punish an incumbent who appears to be underperforming on these issues or taking them for granted. Davis's 67% is durable only if it is actively maintained through specific constituent service and a legislative agenda that speaks to the material concerns of the affluent homeowner majority while demonstrating responsiveness to the younger and renter-majority constituencies that represent the district's demographic future.",
