          { name: "Upper-Middle-Income Economic Concerns", tag: "lean-into", why: "The $76,516 median household income places HD-84 in the upper-middle tier of Oklahoma districts, where voters' material concerns center on healthcare cost management, education quality and funding adequacy, tax fairness, and property value protection rather than abstract economic ideology. These households have achieved relative economic stability and evaluate governance on whether their material position is secure and whether public investment in education and healthcare is adequate. Legislative focus on healthcare affordability for middle-income families, K-12 and higher education funding adequacy, and equitable tax structures demonstrates attentiveness to this constituency's actual economic stakes." },
          { name: "Demographic Gap and Minority Community Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported Census categories account for approximately 95.5% of HD-84's population, leaving roughly 4-5% unaccounted for—likely including Native American residents, Asian residents beyond the 3.48% reported, or mixed-race residents. While this gap is smaller than in some other districts, the absence of clarity on who this population is and what their civic priorities might be represents an engagement blind spot. In an unopposed context, this minority community may have received minimal direct outreach, creating a potential opening for a future challenger to mobilize this constituency around specific unmet needs." },
          { name: "Relying on Unopposed Status as Electoral Strategy", tag: "avoid", why: "The 2024 unopposed victory is a temporary structural advantage that reflects the current absence of a funded opposition candidate, not evidence of durable voter lock-in or genuine electoral dominance. In a district of college-educated professionals at $76,516 income, an organized challenger with specific policy critiques and a demonstrated track record of competence can credibly threaten an incumbent who relies on partisan advantage rather than performance. Treating unopposed status as security rather than as a window to deepen constituent relationships and demonstrate policy effectiveness is strategically dangerous." },
          { name: "Neglecting Renter and Younger Adult Constituencies", tag: "avoid", why: "At 24.8% renters and 37.8 median age, HD-84 contains a meaningful younger and non-property-owning population whose priorities—housing affordability, student loan management, entry-level job quality, and education pipeline—often diverge from older homeowners' concerns. An incumbent focused exclusively on property-tax fairness, school choice, or other homeowner-centric issues may create an opening for a challenger to mobilize younger renters around housing and economic mobility concerns. This constituency deserves specific legislative attention and demonstrated outcomes, not absorption into a homeowner-majority narrative." }
