          { name: "Asian and Hispanic Professional-Class Integration", tag: "careful", why: "The 11.74% Asian and 6.43% Hispanic populations represent professional-class immigrants and growing economic contributors to the district whose specific concerns around professional licensing, immigration policy, and economic mobility can be easily overlooked in a district whose demographic narrative defaults to its white majority. Visible, specific engagement with these communities—not generic 'diversity' positioning, but actual responsiveness to their professional and economic concerns—is both a governing obligation and an electoral signal that the representative understands the district's actual composition and where its growth is coming from." },
          { name: "Ideological Rigidity or Culture-War Positioning", tag: "avoid", why: "A district where 40.6% of adults hold college degrees and the median household income is $98,235 is precisely where culture-war positioning and ideological rigidity produce electoral vulnerability; college-educated professionals evaluate on competence and outcomes, and they have the resources and alternatives to swing away from a representative perceived as prioritizing cultural grievance over fiscal and educational governance. The 36% Democratic opposition in 2024 included swing voters who left the Republican column because they perceived the party as unserious about education, fiscal management, or the specific economic concerns of affluent, professional-class households." },
          { name: "Treating 64% as a Durable Majority Rather Than a Contingent Plurality", tag: "avoid", why: "A 64-36 margin in a college-educated, high-income, suburban district is not a safe Republican seat—it is a working majority held by active alignment between the incumbent's governing record and the constituency's expectations for competence, fiscal discipline, and quality outcomes. Any incumbent or challenger who treats 64% as a durable floor and consolidates only the existing Republican base without addressing the 36% opposition or engaging the swing-prone college-educated constituency will find that margin eroding in the next cycle when national conditions or local governance failures create space for a credible alternative." }
