          { name: "Authentic AIAN Community Engagement & Governance Partnership", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 4.5% demographic gap in a 60-40 district represents approximately 4,500-5,000 residents whose voting behavior is a swing factor in electoral outcomes. Native American voters in working-class precincts are mobilized and demobilized by specific governance attention — tribal sovereignty respect, healthcare equity, economic development partnership — rather than inherited partisan loyalty. An incumbent who treats AIAN constituents as a reliable Democratic subgroup rather than as a community requiring consistent, specific constituent attention and policy response will find that engagement weakness exploited by a credible challenger." },
          { name: "Young Adult Turnout & Electoral Durability", tag: "careful", why: "The 30.7-year median age makes HD-97 a younger-skewing district where electoral participation is more volatile than in older constituencies — young voters mobilize intensely on specific issues (housing, student debt, climate, reproductive rights) and demobilize quickly if they perceive the incumbent as not delivering or as taking their support for granted. The 60% result reflects youth mobilization; any incumbent strategy that assumes young adult turnout will remain steady without ongoing engagement of the economic and social issues that drive youth voting behavior is arithmetically dangerous in a district this young." },
          { name: "Treating 60% as a Safe Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district with 53.2% renters at $55,803 income, 30.7-year median age, and a meaningful AIAN swing population, a 60% result is a current alignment contingent on delivering on economic promises, not a structural Democratic advantage that will hold regardless of incumbent performance. A challenger who addresses young renters' material concerns and engages AIAN constituents as a priority can compress this margin significantly; an incumbent who campaigns defensively or relies on partisan loyalty rather than constituent service will find this margin is not as durable as it appears." },
          { name: "Ignoring Precinct-Level Volatility", tag: "avoid", why: "A 60-40 district can move to 58-42 or closer without losing overall if specific precincts — particularly renter-dense neighborhoods and AIAN-concentrated areas — show lower turnout or candidate preference shift. An incumbent who monitors only the overall margin and does not track whether renter precincts and AIAN communities are maintaining turnout and voting patterns relative to prior cycles will miss early warning signals that the coalition is beginning to fragment, allowing a challenger to organize alternative messaging before the incumbent responds." }
