          "The district's low college attainment (21.4%) and modest income ($59,599) signal a constituency that is economically invested in the status quo but not financially cushioned against disruption—property taxes, healthcare costs, and the availability of local employment are not peripheral concerns but central to household stability. At a 40.8 median age with 75% ownership, this is a district of people who have made long-term residential commitments and are managing ongoing costs of those commitments on incomes that leave limited margin for error. The 25% renter rate represents a meaningful secondary constituency—likely younger adults, service-sector workers, or those priced out of homeownership—whose economic security concerns may differ from the homeowner majority but are equally pressing. Any candidate who wins or holds this seat must demonstrate specific understanding of the property tax and healthcare cost pressures facing the 75% homeowner base and simultaneously acknowledge the housing affordability and wage constraints facing the 25% renter constituency. The Republican margin is built on homeowner alignment, but it is durable only if the homeowner majority perceives that government is protecting their economic position—not pursuing abstract ideology at their expense."
