          { name: "Working-Class Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $59,822 median household income with only 17.9% college attainment, IN-SD-19 is a working-class to lower-middle-class constituency for whom economic security is the primary governance concern, not partisan ideology. Healthcare cost inflation, property tax burden, wage stagnation, and rural service delivery are the material issues that drive voter behavior and constituent satisfaction in this district. An incumbent who demonstrates concrete legislative responsiveness to these concerns — rather than assuming the unopposed result signals durable support — builds the political foundation to withstand a future competitive challenge." },
          { name: "Homeowner Property & Asset Protection", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 74% homeownership rate signals a constituency with significant asset stake in property values, tax burden, and neighborhood stability — making property tax policy, zoning fairness, and housing cost management direct legislative concerns with constituent impact. This is the demographic anchor of the incumbent's current support, and the incumbent who maintains clear legislative commitment to property tax fairness and homeowner economic interests consolidates this base even if opposition emerges in future cycles." },
          { name: "Renter & Younger Household Economic Pressure", tag: "careful", why: "The 26% renter population represents younger households with cost-of-living concerns — housing affordability, transportation, childcare, healthcare access — distinct from the homeowner majority's property-tax-focused interests. At 37.5-year median age, the district contains significant younger-adult households who evaluate on whether government is supporting economic mobility rather than protecting existing homeowner wealth. The incumbent who addresses only homeowner concerns is governing less than three-quarters of the constituency and creating an opening for opposition to mobilize younger, renter-based households around cost-of-living affordability." },
          { name: "Assuming Unopposed Victory Signals Durable Electoral Strength", tag: "avoid", why: "A 2022 unopposed result does not demonstrate that the incumbent has constituent approval or that the seat is politically safe — it demonstrates only that no challenger emerged to test approval. The absence of electoral competition is a vulnerability, not a strength, because it creates institutional blindness to whether economic concerns, service delivery gaps, or demographic shifts have created openings for opposition organizing. An incumbent who treats the unopposed result as a mandate rather than a temporary reprieve is at high risk of being surprised by a credible challenger focusing on concrete economic grievances." },
          { name: "Ideological Positioning Over Economic Responsiveness", tag: "avoid", why: "At 17.9% college attainment and $59,822 income, IN-SD-19 is not an ideologically-driven constituency — it is an economically-focused constituency that evaluates government on whether material concerns (healthcare, wages, taxes, service delivery) are being addressed. An incumbent whose legislative record emphasizes ideological conservatism without demonstrable economic benefit to working-class households creates the political opening that a future challenger can exploit by centering concrete economic grievances. This district votes for candidates who deliver, not candidates who perform ideology." }
