          { name: "Homeowner Economic Security & Education Investment", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 72.2% homeownership and $82,218 median income, the district's base constituency is economically secure but attentive to the quality of public goods—particularly education—that affect property values and children's opportunity. School funding quality, education outcomes, and the relationship between taxes and public services are the material concerns that anchor the homeowner majority's partisan allegiance. Freeman's 60% rests on this coalition; governing away from its priorities creates vulnerability." },
          { name: "4.5% Demographic Gap & AIAN Community Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported Census categories sum to 95.5%, leaving a 4.5% gap that likely represents American Indian / Alaska Native residents. While small, this population can shift a 20-point margin if mobilized around specific governance issues or if a challenger builds authentic relationships where the incumbent has not. In a district this competitive at the educated-voter level, a challenger can use underserved populations as a wedge to fragment the incumbent's coalition." },
          { name: "Treating 60% as a Durable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 60-40 result in a district with 32.8% college attainment and upper-middle-class affluence is not a comfortable majority—it is an equilibrium dependent on the incumbent's positioning relative to educated swing voters' policy concerns. Any incumbent who campaigns as though 60% represents a safe hold, rather than the current balance between a strong homeowner base and an active college-educated swing constituency, will be vulnerable to a challenger who reframes the governing record on healthcare, education, or tax policy." },
          { name: "Ignoring College-Educated Policy Priorities for Abstract Partisan Positioning", tag: "avoid", why: "College-educated voters in upper-middle-class districts evaluate on concrete policy—healthcare access and affordability, education quality, fiscal management—not on abstract ideological framing. A candidate or incumbent who defaults to generic conservative or progressive rhetoric rather than specific answers to the material concerns of professionals earning $80,000+ will lose these swing voters to a challenger with more granular policy engagement. In a district where college-educated voters can shift 5-7 points, this is a governing and electoral failure." }
