          { name: "Black Majority Community Engagement & Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 54.03% Black, IN-SD-34 is a Black-majority district where the unopposed 2024 win reflects incumbent consolidation rather than vulnerability. The real strategic fact is the 23.6% college attainment, 50.6% renter rate, and $48,545 median income—a working-class constituency under material economic pressure where healthcare access, housing affordability, and wage growth are the primary quality-of-life concerns. Any challenger who wants to credibly contest this seat must offer concrete economic alternatives on these issues, not abstract partisan positioning; the incumbent's invulnerability is only real if no credible alternative addresses the economic anxieties of Black working families actually living here." },
          { name: "Renter-Majority Housing & Cost-of-Living Stability", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 50.6% renters—the highest marker of cost-of-living vulnerability in a district earning $48,545 median income—housing stability, eviction prevention, and rent growth management are the direct material concerns of a majority of households. At the median age of 32.7, this is a younger, more mobile constituency than traditional working-class districts, and housing costs consume a larger share of income. A legislative agenda that treats renter rights, affordable housing development, and tenant protections as concrete governance priorities rather than secondary issues connects directly to the lived experience of over half the district's population." },
          { name: "Hispanic & Latino Community Visibility & Service", tag: "careful", why: "At 15.09%, the Hispanic/Latino community represents approximately one in six district residents and is the third-largest demographic group after Black (54.03%) and white (24.91%) residents. In a district where the dominant political narrative centers on Black majority representation, the specific economic and social concerns of the Latino community—workforce participation, language-access services, immigration-status-related healthcare barriers—can become administratively invisible despite their substantial presence. Genuine constituent service to this community requires specific attention and cultural fluency, not absorption into a broader minority-community framing." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Status as Strategic Immunity", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed 2024 win reflects the incumbent's current dominance, not permanent district alignment. In a 54% Black, working-class district with 50.6% renters at $48,545 income and only 23.6% college attainment, the economic conditions that drive electoral vulnerability—unmet needs on housing, healthcare, wages—remain structurally present. Any incumbent who interprets an unopposed win as permission to reduce constituent engagement or who assumes the next challenger will be equally weak is setting up the conditions for a credible primary or general-election challenge; the coalition can be held, but only through active service to the material concerns that the demographics reveal." },
          { name: "Partisan Ideology Positioning Over Constituent Economic Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "IN-SD-34's demographic profile—Black-majority, working-class, renter-heavy, lower college attainment—indicates a constituency that evaluates elected officials primarily on concrete economic delivery: Do they lower housing costs? Do they improve healthcare access? Do they create pathways to wage growth? Positioning based on partisan ideology, national political narratives, or abstract conservative/progressive framing disconnects from the material governance questions that structure daily life for most residents. A representative who substitutes ideological positioning for specific, local economic problem-solving will find that apparent electoral dominance masks growing constituent alienation." }
