          { name: "Post-Graduation Economic Pathway & Student Debt", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 49.6% college-educated population combined with modest $62,789 household income for a young constituency signals significant student debt burden—households with college degrees are not earning substantially more than median income, which indicates either early-career stage, underemployment relative to education, or debt service consuming income that would otherwise translate to household wealth. Healthcare access, wage floors, and whether state policy is expanding economic opportunity for college-educated young adults are material concerns. Current unopposed representation has not articulated a specific legislative agenda on post-graduation economic security, representing a latent vulnerability to any challenger who frames the issue concretely." },
          { name: "Asian, Hispanic & Black Community Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "While smaller populations (6.93% Asian, 4.74% Hispanic, 3.7% Black), these communities represent meaningful constituencies whose civic concerns—immigration, healthcare equity, economic mobility, police accountability—can become invisible when the dominant district narrative centers on educated young white professionals. In a district where the incumbent won unopposed, there is no organized pressure to maintain authentic engagement with minority communities; a challenger who demonstrates specific knowledge of and commitment to these constituencies can activate them substantially. The unopposed victory does not indicate that minority voters are satisfied with current representation, only that they have not had an alternative to evaluate." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Victory as Voter Alignment", tag: "avoid", why: "The 2024 unopposed election is a dangerous source of strategic misreading. It indicates organizational absence or tactical choice by opposition, not voter enthusiasm or satisfaction with current representation. A district where 46.9% of households are renters at median age 30.4 will activate around housing and economic opportunity if presented with a credible alternative; the unopposed result masks this vulnerability. Any current or future representative who interprets the 2024 outcome as evidence of stable voter loyalty rather than as the absence of organized opposition will be structurally unprepared for a competitive challenge." },
          { name: "Abstract Ideological Positioning Without Material Household-Level Policy", tag: "avoid", why: "IN-SD-40 is a constituency that evaluates candidates on concrete policy deliverables affecting their actual economic lives—not on partisan tribal loyalty or ideological messaging divorced from household material concerns. Young renters do not organize around abstract positioning; they organize around housing costs, eviction protection, and whether government is expanding their economic opportunity. A representative who defaults to ideology rather than concrete policy engagement on the issues that drive renter and young adult voter behavior will find the district far more persuadable than the unopposed result suggests, particularly when a challenger articulates specific solutions to the material constraints on household economic security." }
